<description>
Delete the rectangular foreground object that contains the fewest marker-color pixels.

Input structure:
- A two-color set of solid foreground rectangles lies on a single background color.
- One foreground color fills most rectangle cells and the other is a sparse marker color.
- The background is the color with the largest 4-connected component.

Rule:
- Infer the marker color as the less frequent non-background color.
- Count marker-color pixels inside each 4-connected foreground rectangle.
- Recolor the unique rectangle with the smallest marker count to background.

Key insight:
- The object to erase is not selected by position or size; it is the rectangle with
  the least internal marker evidence.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "solid 4-connected foreground rectangles on the background, filled with one color and sparse pixels of the other foreground marker color",
  "transform": "count marker pixels in each rectangle; recolor the unique rectangle with the fewest marker pixels to background and preserve all other pixels"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 15x15 [observed]
  colors:
    background: any single color (largest 4-connected component); 0 in all eval [observed]
    fill: any color != bg (more total pixels than marker); 3 in all eval [observed]
    marker: any color != bg and != fill (fewer total pixels); 9 in all eval [observed]
  rectangles:
    count: 4-6 solid axis-aligned rectangles on background
    dimensions: each side 2-7, area 6-42
    content: filled with fill color + scattered marker pixels
    markers_per_rect: 1-7, each rect has >=1 marker
    marker_density: 4%-33% of rect area
    unique_minimum: exactly one rect has strictly fewer markers than all others
    separation: all pairs separated by >=1 bg pixel (no touching/overlapping)
    background_connectivity: bg forms a single 4-connected component
    may_touch_grid_edges: yes, 2-4 rects per example touch grid boundary [observed]
  transform:
    rule: remove the unique rect with the fewest marker pixels (recolor to bg)
    output_size: same as input

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction
  steps:
    1. Pick grid 15x15, choose 3 distinct colors for bg, fill, marker.
    2. Choose N=4-6 rectangles with random dims (h,w in 2-7 each).
    3. Place rectangles on grid with >=1 pixel gap between all pairs and bg remaining one connected component. Use rejection or greedy packing.
    4. For each rect, scatter marker pixels randomly (density 4-33%, at least 1).
    5. Assign marker counts ensuring exactly one rect has a unique minimum count. Pick the target rect first, give it k markers, then give all others >k markers.
    6. Fill remaining rect cells with fill color, remaining grid cells with bg.
    7. Verify bg is one 4-connected component and marker total < fill total.
```
</generation_spec>
